{"parentUuid":"b3dd9eb2-b6de-4bd1-8946-b4cfa7d11163","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"hook_success","hookName":"PreToolUse:Bash","toolUseID":"toolu_01CKtgVG16CeNi12n9RA98jz","hookEvent":"PreToolUse","content":"","stdout":"{}\n","stderr":"","exitCode":0,"command":"if [ -n \"${CMUX_CLAUDE_HOOK_SPOOL_DIR:-}\" ] && [ -x /bin/zsh ]; then exec /bin/zsh -fc 'LC_ALL=C␤export CMUX_CLAUDE_PID=${CMUX_CLAUDE_PID:-$PPID}␤export CMUX_AGENT_HOOK_INVOKER_PID=$PPID␤cmux_fallback() { { print -rn -- \"$cmux_p\"; /bin/cat; } | /bin/sh -c \"$1\" >&3; exit $?; }␤zmodload zsh/system zsh/datetime zsh/files 2>/dev/null || exec /bin/sh -c \"$1\" >&3␤cmux_p= cmux_c= cmux_e=0␤while (( ${#cmux_p} < 262144 )); do sysread -i 0 -s 65536 cmux_c || { cmux_e=$?; break; }; cmux_p+=$cmux_c; done␤[[ -n ${CMUX_SURFACE_ID:-} && ${CMUX_CLAUDE_HOOKS_DISABLED:-} != 1 ]] || { (( cmux_e == 5 )) || /bin/cat >/dev/null; print -r -- '\\''{}'\\'' >&3; exit 0; }␤cmux_d=$CMUX_CLAUDE_HOOK_SPOOL_DIR␤(( cmux_e == 5 )) && [[ -f $cmux_d/keys ]] || cmux_fallback \"$1\"␤cmux_r=\"cmux-agent-hook-v1\"$'\\''\\n'\\''\"claude\"$'\\''\\n'\\''\"$2\"$'\\''\\n'\\''␤for cmux_k in ${(f)\"$(<$cmux_d/keys)\"}; do (( ${+parameters[$cmux_k]} )) && cmux_r+=\"$cmux_k=${(P)cmux_k}\"$'\\''\\0'\\''; done␤cmux_r+=$'\\''\\0'\\''\"$cmux_p\"␤(( ${#cmux_r} <= 524288 )) || cmux_fallback \"$1\"␤umask 077␤cmux_n=$cmux_d/$epochtime[1].$epochtime[2]-$$␤{ print -rn -- \"$cmux_r\" >| $cmux_n.tmp && mv -f -- $cmux_n.tmp $cmux_n.rec; } 2>/dev/null || { rm -f -- $cmux_n.tmp 2>/dev/null; cmux_fallback \"$1\"; }␤if zsystem flock -t 0 -r -f cmux_l $cmux_d/forwarder.lock 2>/dev/null; then␤  zsystem flock -u $cmux_l␤  rm -- $cmux_n.rec 2>/dev/null && cmux_fallback \"$1\"␤fi␤print -r -- '\\''{}'\\'' >&3' cmux-hook 'cmux_cli=\"${CMUX_CLAUDE_HOOK_CMUX_BIN:-${CMUX_BUNDLED_CLI_PATH:-}}\"; if [ -z \"$cmux_cli\" ] || [ ! -x \"$cmux_cli\" ]; then cmux_cli=\"$(command -v cmux 2>/dev/null || true)\"; fi; agent_pid=\"${CMUX_CLAUDE_PID:-${PPID:-}}\"; if [ -n \"$CMUX_SURFACE_ID\" ] && [ \"$CMUX_CLAUDE_HOOKS_DISABLED\" != \"1\" ] && [ -n \"$cmux_cli\" ]; then if [ -n \"${CMUX_SOCKET_PATH:-}\" ]; then CMUX_CLAUDE_PID=\"$agent_pid\" CMUXTERM_CLI_RESPONSE_TIMEOUT_SEC=0.5 \"$cmux_cli\" --socket \"$CMUX_SOCKET_PATH\" hooks enqueue claude pre-tool-use 2>/dev/null || { cat >/dev/null; echo '\\''{}'\\''; }; else CMUX_CLAUDE_PID=\"$agent_pid\" CMUXTERM_CLI_RESPONSE_TIMEOUT_SEC=0.5 \"$cmux_cli\" hooks enqueue claude pre-tool-use 2>/dev/null || { cat >/dev/null; echo '\\''{}'\\''; }; fi; else cat >/dev/null; echo '\\''{}'\\''; fi' pre-tool-use 3>&1 1>&2; else cmux_cli=\"${CMUX_CLAUDE_HOOK_CMUX_BIN:-${CMUX_BUNDLED_CLI_PATH:-}}\"; if [ -z \"$cmux_cli\" ] || [ ! -x \"$cmux_cli\" ]; then cmux_cli=\"$(command -v cmux 2>/dev/null || true)\"; fi; agent_pid=\"${CMUX_CLAUDE_PID:-${PPID:-}}\"; if [ -n \"$CMUX_SURFACE_ID\" ] && [ \"$CMUX_CLAUDE_HOOKS_DISABLED\" != \"1\" ] && [ -n \"$cmux_cli\" ]; then if [ -n \"${CMUX_SOCKET_PATH:-}\" ]; then CMUX_CLAUDE_PID=\"$agent_pid\" CMUXTERM_CLI_RESPONSE_TIMEOUT_SEC=0.5 \"$cmux_cli\" --socket \"$CMUX_SOCKET_PATH\" hooks enqueue claude pre-tool-use 2>/dev/null || { cat >/dev/null; echo '{}'; }; else CMUX_CLAUDE_PID=\"$agent_pid\" CMUXTERM_CLI_RESPONSE_TIMEOUT_SEC=0.5 \"$cmux_cli\" hooks enqueue claude pre-tool-use 2>/dev/null || { cat >/dev/null; echo '{}'; }; fi; else cat >/dev/null; echo '{}'; fi; fi","durationMs":79},"type":"attachment","uuid":"9bc42ad3-e465-44b1-93a0-b3892946b586","timestamp":"2026-11-12T20:11:35.742Z","session_id":"4fe51ecd-d6cc-423f-a39e-68f5e5b7e45e","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/tacoda/tacoda/bastion","sessionId":"4fe51ecd-d6cc-423f-a39e-68f5e5b7e45e","version":"2.1.285","gitBranch":"main","slug":"parsed-tinkering-blum"}
{"parentUuid":"b3dd9eb2-b6de-4bd1-8946-b4cfa7d11163","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"50f3cf6a-f18b-4b70-b233-30aa8facc040","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01CKtgVG16CeNi12n9RA98jz","type":"tool_result","content":"# Bastion's vision\n\n<!-- voice-register: informational -->\n<!-- voice-english: ste -->\n\nBastion is an agent control plane. You define workflows and send requests,\nand Bastion fulfills each request as a job: agents work through the\nworkflow's steps, under the rules you set on each step. Jobs and workflows\nare the two ideas everything else serves. The cost is plain: a job does only\nwhat its workflow says, so a check you take out of a workflow is gone.\nBastion runs on your machine, on a remote server, or on both at once.\n\n## Jobs and workflows\n\nA workflow is a series of steps on a canvas. A step is a prompt, one of the\nrepository's agents or skills, or a shell script, and each step has one\ntext to edit. Each step also carries its own settings:\n\n- **Its dial:** a sun when a person decides, a moon when it runs on its\n  own.\n- **Its model and its budget:** how often its failure may send the work\n  back, and the most its turns may spend.\n- **Its rules:** the constraints its agent works under, and the calls the\n  agent may make.\n- **Its links:** the results it passes on, and the step a failure goes to.\n\nA job is one request run through one workflow. It runs the steps in order,\nwaits where a step's dial says a person decides, and ends with its branch\nready, merged or left for you. Bastion ships the common workflows with their\ntext filled in, and you can change every step of them.\n\n## Rules move onto the workflow\n\nBastion used to split its rules into two layers. The harness bound an\nagent's calls. The factory bound the work around them: what a step may do,\nand when a change lands. Both now live more and more on a workflow and its\nsteps, and Bastion applies them as each step runs. I think the two layers\nwill collapse into one, with Bastion applying whatever the workflow and its\nsteps set. The split may still be useful, though, and that isn't settled;\nthe next few workflows people write will settle it.\n\nA repository brings its own rules too: its charter, in `AGENTS.md` and\n`.agents/settings.json`. A turn may read the charter but may not change it.\n\n## Two levels: each server, and all of them\n\nEvery page shows one server, the one you pick at the top. Some pages can\nalso show every server at once, and those are the ones about records.\n\n**Records, per server or across all.** Jobs, Audit Log, Costs, Evals and\nVerification read one server, or every server you added, side by side. All\nservers is one more choice in the server picker, so you filter by server the\nsame way on each of these pages. You open a job, an entry or a turn and act\non it wherever it lives.\n\n**Rules, one server at a time.** Workflows, Constraints, Capabilities,\nPermissions, Engine and Settings belong to the server you pick. Bastion\nholds the rules as its harness, and a workflow applies them: each rules\npage turns an item on for every workflow, or for the workflows and steps\nyou pick. A step's Rules tab lists what applies there, read-only. A rules\npage never mixes two servers, because each server's rules shape only its\nown jobs, and a sum of two rule sets means nothing.\n\n## What a server holds\n\nEach server is a control plane of its own, and everything that shapes it\nlives with it.\n\n- **Workspaces:** the repositories it works in, each with its checks, its\n  setup, its VM image, and what happens to a job's branch at the end.\n- **Workflows:** its series of steps, each step with its settings.\n- **Rules:** constraints, what the agent must keep; capabilities, what it\n  may use; and permissions, what the gate lets a call do. A constraint is\n  feedforward, told to the agent before it acts, or feedback, a check, hook\n  or reviewer on what it did. Each grant is ask or allowed. Each item\n  applies at every workflow, or at the workflows and steps you pick. The\n  charter each workspace's repository brings applies too, and changes in\n  the repository.\n- **Settings:** its model providers, its people and their roles, its\n  notifications, and its secrets.\n- **Records:** its jobs, its audit log, its evals, its costs, and what\n  landed.\n\nThese are live controls, not a configuration file you edit and restart. A\nserver runs on the iii engine, and each value you change in the app changes\nhow that engine works for the next call or the next job: which model a turn\nruns on, which calls an agent may make, which image a VM boots, what a step\ndoes. Bastion's job is to turn the values you set into how iii behaves.\n\nThe app itself keeps one thing: the list of servers you added. That list is\nthe only setting that belongs to you and not to a server.\n\n## Local and remote\n\nA local server starts with the app and needs no credentials. A remote\nserver needs a token the server issued you, over TLS. Either kind runs the\nsame control plane, so a workflow written on one works on the other. A job\nruns on one server; your jobs can run on many.\n\n## What does not change\n\n- Agent code runs in a VM, never on your machine. A step reaches the\n  network only when its workflow says so, with the secrets it names.\n- Every action names who took it, in the server's audit log.\n- A seeded default is a starting point, never a lock.\n\n## Not this\n\nNot an editor: the only code you read in Bastion is the change a job\nproposes. Not a web app.\n\n## Next\n\nCopy the Short workflow, give its build step its own model and its own\ngrants, and send it one request. The job page shows what each step did\nunder those settings.\n     997 VISION.md\ncommit-msg\npost-commit\npost-rewrite\npre-push\nprepare-commit-msg","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"38126456-6187-4248-8fe0-3b32f4bc9bae","timestamp":"2026-11-12T20:11:36.548Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"# Bastion's vision\n\n<!-- voice-register: informational -->\n<!-- voice-english: ste -->\n\nBastion is an agent control plane. You define workflows and send requests,\nand Bastion fulfills each request as a job: agents work through the\nworkflow's steps, under the rules you set on each step. Jobs and workflows\nare the two ideas everything else serves. The cost is plain: a job does only\nwhat its workflow says, so a check you take out of a workflow is gone.\nBastion runs on your machine, on a remote server, or on both at once.\n\n## Jobs and workflows\n\nA workflow is a series of steps on a canvas. A step is a prompt, one of the\nrepository's agents or skills, or a shell script, and each step has one\ntext to edit. Each step also carries its own settings:\n\n- **Its dial:** a sun when a person decides, a moon when it runs on its\n  own.\n- **Its model and its budget:** how often its failure may send the work\n  back, and the most its turns may spend.\n- **Its rules:** the constraints its agent works under, and the calls the\n  agent may make.\n- **Its links:** the results it passes on, and the step a failure goes to.\n\nA job is one request run through one workflow. It runs the steps in order,\nwaits where a step's dial says a person decides, and ends with its branch\nready, merged or left for you. Bastion ships the common workflows with their\ntext filled in, and you can change every step of them.\n\n## Rules move onto the workflow\n\nBastion used to split its rules into two layers. The harness bound an\nagent's calls. The factory bound the work around them: what a step may do,\nand when a change lands. Both now live more and more on a workflow and its\nsteps, and Bastion applies them as each step runs. I think the two layers\nwill collapse into one, with Bastion applying whatever the workflow and its\nsteps set. The split may still be useful, though, and that isn't settled;\nthe next few workflows people write will settle it.\n\nA repository brings its own rules too: its charter, in `AGENTS.md` and\n`.agents/settings.json`. A turn may read the charter but may not change it.\n\n## Two levels: each server, and all of them\n\nEvery page shows one server, the one you pick at the top. Some pages can\nalso show every server at once, and those are the ones about records.\n\n**Records, per server or across all.** Jobs, Audit Log, Costs, Evals and\nVerification read one server, or every server you added, side by side. All\nservers is one more choice in the server picker, so you filter by server the\nsame way on each of these pages. You open a job, an entry or a turn and act\non it wherever it lives.\n\n**Rules, one server at a time.** Workflows, Constraints, Capabilities,\nPermissions, Engine and Settings belong to the server you pick. Bastion\nholds the rules as its harness, and a workflow applies them: each rules\npage turns an item on for every workflow, or for the workflows and steps\nyou pick. A step's Rules tab lists what applies there, read-only. A rules\npage never mixes two servers, because each server's rules shape only its\nown jobs, and a sum of two rule sets means nothing.\n\n## What a server holds\n\nEach server is a control plane of its own, and everything that shapes it\nlives with it.\n\n- **Workspaces:** the repositories it works in, each with its checks, its\n  setup, its VM image, and what happens to a job's branch at the end.\n- **Workflows:** its series of steps, each step with its settings.\n- **Rules:** constraints, what the agent must keep; capabilities, what it\n  may use; and permissions, what the gate lets a call do. A constraint is\n  feedforward, told to the agent before it acts, or feedback, a check, hook\n  or reviewer on what it did. Each grant is ask or allowed. Each item\n  applies at every workflow, or at the workflows and steps you pick. The\n  charter each workspace's repository brings applies too, and changes in\n  the repository.\n- **Settings:** its model providers, its people and their roles, its\n  notifications, and its secrets.\n- **Records:** its jobs, its audit log, its evals, its costs, and what\n  landed.\n\nThese are live controls, not a configuration file you edit and restart. A\nserver runs on the iii engine, and each value you change in the app changes\nhow that engine works for the next call or the next job: which model a turn\nruns on, which calls an agent may make, which image a VM boots, what a step\ndoes. Bastion's job is to turn the values you set into how iii behaves.\n\nThe app itself keeps one thing: the list of servers you added. That list is\nthe only setting that belongs to you and not to a server.\n\n## Local and remote\n\nA local server starts with the app and needs no credentials. A remote\nserver needs a token the server issued you, over TLS. Either kind runs the\nsame control plane, so a workflow written on one works on the other. A job\nruns on one server; your jobs can run on many.\n\n## What does not change\n\n- Agent code runs in a VM, never on your machine. A step reaches the\n  network only when its workflow says so, with the secrets it names.\n- Every action names who took it, in the server's audit log.\n- A seeded default is a starting point, never a lock.\n\n## Not this\n\nNot an editor: the only code you read in Bastion is the change a job\nproposes. Not a web app.\n\n## Next\n\nCopy the Short workflow, give its build step its own model and its own\ngrants, and send it one request. The job page shows what each step did\nunder those settings.\n     997 VISION.md\ncommit-msg\npost-commit\npost-rewrite\npre-push\nprepare-commit-msg","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"serverClassifierContext":{"request":"155a6cd5-4670-4b33-8764-ddbca4aa4b46","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/Users/tacoda/tacoda/bastion","root":"/Users/tacoda/tacoda/bastion","branch":"main","default_branch":"main","status":{"clean":true,"counts":{"staged":0,"modified":0,"untracked":null,"untracked_normal":0},"porcelain":null,"truncated":false},"visibility":{"origin":null,"push_remote":"origin","remotes":[],"visibility_cache":[]}},"live_cwd":"/Users/tacoda/tacoda/bastion","platform":"macos"}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"b3dd9eb2-b6de-4bd1-8946-b4cfa7d11163","session_id":"4fe51ecd-d6cc-423f-a39e-68f5e5b7e45e","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/tacoda/tacoda/bastion","sessionId":"4fe51ecd-d6cc-423f-a39e-68f5e5b7e45e","version":"2.1.285","gitBranch":"main","slug":"parsed-tinkering-blum"}
